# What `vsce package` leaves out.
#
# The package holds what VS Code reads at runtime: the manifest, the bundled
# `dist/extension.js`, the language configuration, the TextMate grammar, the
# README, the licence and the notice. Everything a developer needs to build or
# test the extension is out, and so is every source map: a `.map` next to the
# bundle is 1.6 MB, it names the absolute paths of the machine that built it,
# and nothing in the editor opens it.
#
# `server/**` is not excluded. A platform package carries the server at
# `server/jasmin-lsp` beside `server/SHA256SUMS`, and the universal package
# carries neither; the difference is whether the directory was staged before
# `vsce` ran, not a line here. An exclusion of `**/*.map` still leaves the
# bundled server alone, because it is an executable with no extension.
#
# The test cache is named twice on purpose. `.vscode-test/**` leaves out the
# contents of a real download, and the bare `.vscode-test` leaves out the path
# itself, which is what a worktree that links one shared download puts here: a
# symlink is not a directory, so the first pattern does not match it and `vsce
# ls` would list the link as a file of the package.
.artifacts/**
.vscode/**
.vscode-test
.vscode-test/**
.vscode-test-web/**
coverage/**
fixtures/**
node_modules/**
scripts/**
test/**
tests/**
src/**
**/*.map
**/*.ts
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.editorconfig
esbuild.mjs
pnpm-lock.yaml
pnpm-workspace.yaml
tsconfig.json
